yousub (2.7.2) stable; urgency=medium

  * Development tooling only: the extension behaves exactly as 2.7.1. The
    build ships the same files; this release adds the checks around them.
  * Coverage now measures the shipped scripts instead of only the test files.
    Every test harness names the file it loads, so V8 attributes executed
    lines to drive.js, content/content.js, popup/popup.js and the rest, and
    npm run coverage enforces line, branch and function minimums.
  * ESLint (flat config) and tsc --noEmit with checkJs run in CI, so an
    undefined cross-file global, a misspelled chrome.* API or a property that
    does not exist fails the build. types/yousub.d.ts is type-only and never
    reaches the CRX, the ZIP or the .deb.
  * Fixes found by the new checks: a thrown error keeps its original cause,
    an unnecessary escape was removed from the subscribe-label regex, and a
    click handler no longer depends on short-circuit evaluation.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <arobidsh@proton.me>  Thu, 26 Nov 2026 06:30:00 +0000

yousub (2.7.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Keep the saved Google sign-in across extension updates: yousubOAuthTokens
    was missing from the list of storage keys the service worker keeps, so
    every update deleted the refresh token and stopped the background sync
    until the person signed in again. A copy kept in the browser session area
    also restores a sign-in that an earlier build deleted.
  * Error messages point at controls that exist: the side panel has no
    "Sign in" button and no "Client secret" field, so the messages now say
    to click the configuration-file button and choose the file again, and the
    consent text no longer quotes the narrower drive.file permission.
  * A token that Google rejects is discarded before the message asks for a
    new sign-in, so the same dead token is not replayed on every sync.
  * The panel listens to storage changes once instead of three times, and its
    sign-in record is no longer labelled as an email key.
  * The manifest asks only for the hosts the extension contacts: the Drive
    page and *.googleusercontent.com were never requested by the code.
  * Install debian/copyright as /usr/share/doc/yousub/copyright and keep
    the build's md5sums complete; Policy requires the first, and the publish
    job refuses a package lintian rejects (no-copyright-file,
    file-missing-in-md5sums, extra-license-file).
  * Stop writing to /etc/opt, which the FHS reserves for add-on software, and
    declare the Chromium registration in /etc as a configuration file
    (dir-or-file-in-etc-opt, file-in-etc-not-marked-as-conffile).
  * Give the 2.7.1 entry its own date and shorten the synopsis so it no
    longer starts with the package name (latest-changelog-entry-without-new-date,
    description-starts-with-package-name).

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <arobidsh@proton.me>  Wed, 25 Nov 2026 22:00:00 +0000

yousub (2.7.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Configuration is one JSON file picked once in the side panel
    ({"yousub-file", "client_id", "client_secret"}) instead of a folder that was
    scanned for several files; its contents are cached in the extension storage
    and are never read again, not even after the browser restarts.
  * The Drive scope is now the full auth/drive scope: drive.file cannot open a
    YouTube.json the user created, which Google answers with 404.
  * A client_secret in the configuration keeps a refresh token so the sync runs in
    the background; without one the implicit flow keeps the token in memory and
    renews it silently from the Google session.
  * Every problem in the configuration file is reported at once, and a file that
    fails validation never replaces a working configuration.
  * Every Google request is bounded to 20 seconds.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <arobidsh@proton.me>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000

yousub (2.6.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Sign in to Google with your own account (OAuth 2.0) instead of a service
    account: the side panel now takes an OAuth client ID and secret plus the
    YouTube.json file link, and the history file is read and written with the
    drive.file scope only.
  * The Google settings form replaces the configuration folder picker; a folder
    holding the OAuth client file is no longer read.
  * The archive keeps a single suite (stable) and serves its indices by hash
    (Acquire-By-Hash) with an advisory Valid-Until date.
  * The package control file is generated by dpkg-gencontrol, and CI runs
    lintian on the built package.

